  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality ${x}^{2}\\ge 5x+14$ and express the solution set in interval notation.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]\\,\u22c3\\,[$\n{{field-3}}$,\\,\\infty )$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the terms to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\ge 5x+14 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x-14 & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}-5x-14$ lies **above** the $x$-axis or touches this axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x-14 & =0 \\\\ (x-7)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-2$ and $x=7,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/628325ae599d6b004fdabe85319ec9f8acd701c76b776cc4dea6b321cb554415.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-5x-14\\ge 0,\n$$\n\n \nwe look for the part of the parabola that is **above** the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\le -2$ or $x\\ge 7.$\n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\u22c3[7,\\infty ).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
